.TH CCOL_MEMPOOL_USED_COUNT 3 "2026-09-17" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_mempool_used_count \- report how many pool elements are in use
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cmempool.h>

size_t ccol_mempool_used_count(ccol_mempool *mp);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns the number of pool-owned elements that the pool holds out to
callers now. An element that sits in the cache of a thread has left the
shared free list, but the pool gave it to nobody, so the call does not
count it as used. It does not count a dynamic fallback entry either; see
ccol_mempool_dynamic_allocs_count(3) for those.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
mp                        Memory pool to query (must not be NULL)
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns the number of elements in use now
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The count is exact whenever the pool is quiet. If other threads allocate
or free while this call runs, the result is a snapshot that can already
be stale when the call returns, which is inherent in a count of
something that moves.

On a quiet pool the result never exceeds
ccol_mempool_total_capacity(). Under concurrent allocation on a pool
with a cache for each thread, it can sit a few entries above that for a
short time, but never above the physical slot count that
ccol_mempool_allocated_bytes(3) reports. See
ccol_mempool_total_capacity(3) for why.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
Thread-safe when the pool was created with single_threaded = false.
The call takes the lock of the pool and walks the cache of each thread,
which makes it a cold-path call: do not use it inside a hot loop.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_mempool_total_capacity (3),
.BR ccol_mempool_dynamic_allocs_count (3),
.BR ccol_mempool_create (3)
